  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1228 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nAn upper level trough will track across the Southern Plains Tuesday. \nJust ahead of this trough, a broad and extended baroclinic zone will \nmove north to south across the area, bolstering the cold airmass. As \nthe trough rides atop this cooler air, southerly 700mb flow will \ndirect moisture over that air and the isentropic lift provides an \nopportunity for isolated to scattered light rain showers throughout \nthe day beneath overcast skies. A few additional models are hinting \nat a band of light to moderate showers moving across the Hill \nCountry and potentially the Austin metro Tuesday afternoon/evening \njust ahead of a 500mb PV anomaly. These showers would be in a \nmeteorologically interesting position as temperatures at the top of \nthe stratiform cloud deck and wet bulb temperatures below the clouds \ncould be cool enough to support a brief mix of ice pellets (or small \nchunks of mostly melted snow flurries) within the generally rain-\nfavoring environment. No accumulation or impactful weather is \nexpected as surface temperatures during this time are forecast to \nbe well above freezing.\n\nDry and cold air will spread across the area behnd Tuesday's clouds \nand light showers, setting up the coldest temperatures we've seen so \nfar this season. Models have steadily trended colder for projected \novernight lows Wednesday and Thanksgiving Day, with freezing \ntemperatures becoming more likely for the Hill Country and \npotentially in rural portions of the I-35 corridor. Thanksgiving \nmorning in particular looks especially ripe for strong radiational \ncooling with deep layer dryness, and it's no surprise that it's the \ncoldest night in the latest statistical guidance. The lowest \ntemperatures projected have mainly fallen in the 28 to 32 degree \nrange. Outside of the freezing areas, most will still cool into the \n30s. The medium range guidance generally depicts a ridge over our \narea on Thursday, which favors a sunny Thanksgiving with highs in \nthe 50s to near 60, cooler than average for late November. \n\nTroughing remains in place across the central US entering the \nweekend, with northwesterly mid-level flow maintained over Texas in \nthe ensemble mean fields. As ridging builds in its wake over the \nwestern US, a gradual warming trend is depicted. However, the \nnightly temperature forecasts remain cool as conditions remain\ndry.",
